1
00:00:02,100 --> 00:00:03,600
Keeping two databases consistent. Dual writes, the transactional outbox, and a version guard

2
00:00:06,500 --> 00:00:09,000
Staff resolve ticket 1008. Step 1: update PostgreSQL and commit.

3
00:00:09,500 --> 00:00:12,500
Step 2: update MongoDB... and the call times out.

4
00:00:13,000 --> 00:00:15,500
The error does not undo the commit. The two stores now disagree.

5
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:18,500
Swap the order? Then MongoDB can change while PostgreSQL never does.

6
00:00:19,000 --> 00:00:23,400
A PostgreSQL transaction covers PostgreSQL statements, not a network call.

7
00:00:23,900 --> 00:00:27,900
Attempt 1: update the ticket, then fail before inserting the event.

8
00:00:28,400 --> 00:00:30,400
The transaction rolls back: ticket still open v16, outbox still empty.

9
00:00:30,900 --> 00:00:32,900
Attempt 2: ticket update and event insert, then COMMIT.

10
00:00:33,400 --> 00:00:36,400
Both commit or neither does. No new status without its event.

11
00:00:36,900 --> 00:00:40,900
The outbox is just a table of outgoing work, waiting to be delivered.

12
00:00:41,400 --> 00:00:45,200
A relay publishes unsent events. A consumer applies them to MongoDB.

13
00:00:45,700 --> 00:00:49,500
These are responsibilities, not products: one small process can do both jobs.

14
00:00:50,000 --> 00:00:55,500
Every arrow after the commit can fail. The outbox is not exactly-once delivery.

15
00:00:56,000 --> 00:00:57,500
Each event carries the COMPLETE status at a version set by PostgreSQL.

16
00:00:58,000 --> 00:01:02,800
Guard ON: deliveries 17, 17 again, 18, then a late 16.

17
00:01:03,300 --> 00:01:07,200
The duplicate and the late event change nothing. Final: closed v18.

18
00:01:07,700 --> 00:01:12,500
Guard OFF, same events in the same order.

19
00:01:13,000 --> 00:01:15,000
The late v16 moves the ticket backward: closed → open.

20
00:01:15,500 --> 00:01:20,400
Setting a status twice is repeat-safe (idempotent). It still needs an ordering rule.

21
00:01:20,900 --> 00:01:22,400
With two consumers, check-then-write in app code races.

22
00:01:22,900 --> 00:01:27,200
In MongoDB, the filter and the update on one document are one atomic operation.

23
00:01:27,700 --> 00:01:30,700
Delta events need a different policy: ordered replay, gap detection, or rebuild.

24
00:01:32,300 --> 00:01:42,300
Remember: A dual write can commit in one store and fail in the other. Outbox: the change and its event commit together, or neither does. Delivery after commit can repeat or arrive late. Apply a complete-state event only if its version is newer. Repeat-safe is not the same as correctly ordered.

